<p dir=3D"auto">The <strong>Critical Rendering Path (CRP)</strong> is the s=
equence of steps the browser takes to convert HTML, CSS, and JavaScript int=
o pixels on the screen.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>The 6 steps in order:</strong></p>
<ol dir=3D"auto">
<li>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Constructing the DOM Tree</strong> =E2=80=94 Browse=
r parses HTML and builds the Document Object Model (tree of nodes)</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Constructing the CSSOM Tree</strong> =E2=80=94 Brow=
ser parses CSS and builds the CSS Object Model (style rules tree)</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Running JavaScript</strong> =E2=80=94 JS is a <stro=
ng>parser-blocking resource</strong>; the browser stops HTML parsing to exe=
cute it (unless <code>async</code>/<code>defer</code> is used)</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Creating the Render Tree</strong> =E2=80=94 DOM + C=
SSOM are combined; only visible elements are included (e.g., <code>display:=
none</code> nodes are excluded)</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Generating the Layout</strong> =E2=80=94 Browser ca=
lculates the exact size and position of each element on the screen (also ca=
lled "reflow")</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Painting</strong> =E2=80=94 Browser fills in the ac=
tual pixels =E2=80=94 text, colors, images, borders, shadows</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Optimizing the CRP improve=
s page load performance. Key techniques include:</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>Place <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> stylesheets in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code=
> (avoid render blocking)</li>
<li>Place <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags at bottom of <code>&lt;body&gt;<=
/code>, or use <code>async</code>/<code>defer</code></li>
<li>Minify CSS and JS</li>
<li>Reduce render-blocking resources</li>
</ul>
