<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Benefits of SSR over CSR:</strong></p>
<ol dir=3D"auto">
<li>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Faster initial page load</strong> =E2=80=94 The ser=
ver sends fully rendered HTML, so the browser displays content immediately =
without waiting for JavaScript bundles to download and execute.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Better SEO</strong> =E2=80=94 Search engine crawler=
s receive complete HTML content directly, making it easier to index pages a=
ccurately. CSR often sends an empty <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> that crawlers=
 may not fully process.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>No content blocking on slow networks</strong> =E2=
=80=94 In CSR, users on slow connections wait for JS to load before seeing =
anything. With SSR, content is visible as soon as HTML is parsed.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Co-located API calls are faster</strong> =E2=80=94 =
The server fetches data internally (low latency), renders it, and ships the=
 final HTML =E2=80=94 versus CSR where the browser must make separate API c=
alls after JS loads.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Trade-offs to consider:</strong></p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table data-catalyst=3D""><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>SSR</th>
<th>CSR</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Initial load</td>
<td>Faster (content in HTML)</td>
<td>Slower (waits for JS)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Subsequent navigation</td>
<td>Slower (full page requests)</td>
<td>Faster (only data fetched)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Server load</td>
<td>Higher</td>
<td>Lower</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>SEO</td>
<td>Better out of the box</td>
<td>Requires extra effort</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Interactivity</td>
<td>Requires hydration step</td>
<td>Immediate after JS loads</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<p dir=3D"auto">Modern frameworks like Next.js and Nuxt.js combine both app=
roaches =E2=80=94 using SSR for the initial load and CSR for subsequent nav=
igation =E2=80=94 to get the best of both worlds.</p>
