<p dir=3D"auto">A <strong>layout engine</strong> (also called a rendering e=
ngine) is the core software component of a browser responsible for parsing =
HTML/CSS and rendering the visual output on screen.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>The three active engines today:</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Blink</strong> (Google, 2013 =E2=80=94 forked from =
WebKit)</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>Used by: Chrome, Edge (since 2020), Opera, Brave, Samsung Internet, Viv=
aldi</li>
<li>Powers the majority of web traffic today</li>
<li>Developed by Google + contributors via the Chromium project</li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>WebKit</strong> (Apple, 2003 =E2=80=94 forked from =
KHTML)</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>Used by: Safari on macOS/iOS, and <strong>all</strong> browsers on iOS =
(Apple mandates WebKit on iOS App Store)</li>
<li>The original engine Chrome was based on before Google forked it into Bl=
ink</li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Gecko</strong> (Mozilla, 1998)</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>Used by: Firefox, Thunderbird, SeaMonkey, Waterfox</li>
<li>The only major independent engine not owned by a big tech company</li>
<li>Uses a separate JS engine called <strong>SpiderMonkey</strong></li>
</ul>
