<p dir=3D"auto">An <strong>HTML preprocessor</strong> is a tool that lets y=
ou write HTML in an extended syntax with features like variables, loops, co=
nditionals, and mixins =E2=80=94 then compiles it down to standard HTML tha=
t browsers can read.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Why Use One?</strong></p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li><strong>Avoid repetition</strong> =E2=80=94 define layouts/partials onc=
e, reuse everywhere</li>
<li><strong>Dynamic content</strong> =E2=80=94 inject server-side data (fro=
m DB, API, etc.) into templates</li>
<li><strong>Cleaner syntax</strong> =E2=80=94 less boilerplate than raw HTM=
L</li>
<li><strong>Inheritance</strong> =E2=80=94 extend base layouts with child t=
emplates</li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Popular HTML Templating Languages</strong></p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>Pug (formerly Jade)</li>
<li>Haml</li>
<li>ERB (Embedded Ruby)</li>
<li>Handlebars</li>
<li>Jinja2</li>
<li>Liquid</li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"auto">The key distinction from plain HTML is <strong>server-side =
rendering of dynamic data</strong> =E2=80=94 the preprocessor fills in vari=
ables, loops through data, and outputs fully-formed HTML before it ever rea=
ches the browser.</p>
