<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Server-Sent Events (SSE)</strong> is a server push =
technology that enables a server to send real-time updates to the browser o=
ver a single, long-lived HTTP connection. Unlike WebSockets, SSE is <strong=
>one-directional</strong> =E2=80=94 server to client only =E2=80=94 and aut=
omatically reconnects if the connection is lost.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>When to use SSE vs WebSockets:</strong></p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table data-catalyst=3D""><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>SSE</th>
<th>WebSocket</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Direction</td>
<td>Server =E2=86=92 Client only</td>
<td>Full duplex (both directions)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Protocol</td>
<td>HTTP/HTTPS</td>
<td>ws:// / wss://</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Auto-reconnect</td>
<td>Yes (built-in)</td>
<td>No (must implement manually)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Format</td>
<td>Text (UTF-8)</td>
<td>Text or binary</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Use case</td>
<td>Live feeds, notifications, dashboards</td>
<td>Chat, gaming, collaboration</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
